            <img src="/images/posting/mommy-posting-sites.png" width="1200" height="630" alt={title} loading="eager" />
          </figure>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-body">
        <div class="container article-shell">
          <div class="prose pillar-body">
            <p class="prose-lede">Parenting publishing splits into four tiers, and where you can land a byline depends on the credential or the story behind it.</p>

            <p>The big pregnancy and baby brands are medically reviewed and staff-written. The parenting magazines take contributions with the right angle. The community sites want real parent voices. The independent blogs are open. The list of 100 is below, grouped by tier.</p>

            <h2 id="tier">Pick your tier first</h2>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Tier</th><th>Examples</th><th>How you get in</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>Pregnancy &amp; baby brands</td><td>BabyCenter, What to Expect, The Bump, Pampers, Huggies, HealthyChildren</td><td>Not open guest posts. A byline from an OB-GYN, pediatrician, IBCLC or licensed therapist</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Parenting magazines</td><td>Parents, Today's Parent, Mother.ly, Fatherly, Mumsnet, Kidspot</td><td>Freelance and contributor pitches; an angle the staff missed, first-hand with expertise</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Mom-community sites</td><td>Scary Mommy, CafeMom, Romper, Grown and Flown, The Every Mom</td><td>Real parent voices; personal essays, relatable how-tos, community stories</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Independent parenting blogs</td><td>Mama Natural, Aha! Parenting, Twiniversity, Your Modern Family, ParentMap</td><td>Open to contributions; niche expertise, personal experience, practical guides</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="metrics">What the metrics mean</h2>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>Metric</th><th>What it is</th><th>How much it matters</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr><td>Domain Rating (DR)</td><td>Ahrefs' 0 to 100 score for a whole site's backlink profile</td><td>A useful filter, not a verdict</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Domain Authority (DA)</td><td>Moz's equivalent 0 to 100 score</td><td>Same idea as DR</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Page Authority (PA)</td><td>Moz's score for one specific page</td><td>Matters once you know which page your post will live on</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>URL Rating (UR)</td><td>Ahrefs' score for one specific URL</td><td>Same purpose as PA</td></tr>
                  <tr><td>Monthly Traffic</td><td>Estimated organic visits per month</td><td>The most honest signal that a link will be seen</td></tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Here is the deal:</strong> the original export was padded with tiny, inactive mom blogs. Those were removed. Parenting is soft-YMYL, so a placement with a real expert byline on a reviewed site is worth far more than a link on a blog that has not posted since 2019.</p></div>

            <h2 id="list">The 100 parenting sites</h2>
            <p>Domain Rating, Domain Authority, Page Authority, URL Rating and estimated monthly traffic. Every domain was checked live in September 2026; shut-down and inactive sites were replaced with live equivalents. Metrics are third-party estimates and shift over time.</p>
            <figure>
              <table>
                <thead>
                  <tr><th>#</th><th>Website</th><th>DR</th><th>DA</th><th>PA</th><th>UR</th><th>Monthly traffic</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  {SITES.map((s) => (
                    <tr>
                      <td>{s.n}</td>
                      <td>{s.site}</td>
                      <td>{s.dr}</td>
                      <td>{s.da}</td>
                      <td>{s.pa}</td>
                      <td>{s.ur}</td>
                      <td>{s.traffic}</td>
                    </tr>
                  ))}
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </figure>

            <h2 id="pitch">The pitch that works for parenting editors</h2>
            <ol>
              <li><strong>Lead with the credential or the moment.</strong> "As a pediatric sleep consultant" or "the night my toddler's fever hit 104 and what the ER taught me" earns the read.</li>
              <li><strong>Pitch the angle the staff missed.</strong> Not "how to sleep train." Something like "why sleep training advice fails for the second child."</li>
              <li><strong>Bring your own detail.</strong> Real anecdotes, photos of your own kids where relevant, a framework you actually use.</li>
              <li><strong>Match the tier's standard.</strong> Health and development pieces need an expert author or reviewer. Community pieces need an honest, specific voice.</li>
              <li><strong>Send one clip</strong> in a similar register and your short bio.</li>
              <li><strong>Disclose any product or program</strong> you have a stake in.</li>
            </ol>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>The test:</strong> would an editor run this if you had nothing to sell? If yes, pitch it. If it is an ad for your course, it goes in the bin.</p></div>

            <h2 id="mistakes">What not to do</h2>
            <ul class="check-list">
              <li>Pitch a generic "10 tips for new moms" listicle with no author credential.</li>
              <li>Make feeding, sleep or child-health claims with no expert attached.</li>
              <li>Send a personal essay with no specific detail. Parenting editors get thousands.</li>
              <li>Stuff affiliate links, or point exact-match anchors ("best baby monitor") at your store.</li>
              <li>Use AI-generated parenting content. It reads as generic and hollow immediately.</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="callout"><p><strong>Bottom line:</strong> a few placements on reviewed brands and community sites with real readers will do more for rankings and trust than a long list of dormant mom-blog links. Use this list to find your few.</p></div>
          </div>

          <aside class="article-toc">
            <h4>On this page</h4>
            <ul>
              {toc.map((item) => (
                <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.text}</a></li>
